(ned MEASURE SHOULDER -- the second half of a tool's profile.)
(Operator 2026-08-12: "it moves the bit so that the tool diameter is 1mm)
(from the puck and probes down so that the shoulder makes contact. now, we)
(will have a full profile of the bit.")

(WHAT IT MEASURES: the height of the shoulder above the tool tip. The nose)
(is already known -- MEASURE TOOL OFFSET wrote it into the tool table as the)
(Z offset -- so probing the shoulder against the same puck top gives the)
(distance between the two, which is what a collision check needs.)

(THE GEOMETRY. The cutter must MISS the puck and the shoulder must LAND on)
(it, so the spindle is offset sideways by:)
(    puck radius + cutting radius + 1 mm)
(The 1 mm is the operator's clearance between the cutter and the puck edge.)
(HOW MUCH NUT LANDS ON THE PUCK. The nut's inner edge sits at offset minus)
(shoulder radius from the puck centre, so the overlap onto the puck top is)
(puck radius minus that = shoulder radius - cutting radius - clearance.)
(THE PUCK DIAMETER CANCELS OUT: the only thing that matters is that the nut)
(out-reaches the cutter by more than the clearance gap.)
(2026-08-13: this used to read shoulder radius MINUS THE WHOLE OFFSET, which)
(is the overhang past the puck's CENTRE, not its rim. It demanded a nut wide)
(enough to cover half the puck -- 36.8 mm for a 3/8 cutter -- so T9 in an)
(ER16-28 was refused with "the cutter is too big" while the nut was in fact)
(overlapping the puck by 8.235 mm.)

(PUCK GEOMETRY comes from the calibration block, not from a constant here:)
(#3045/#3046 centre, #3047 top. Diameter measured 2026-08-03 as 25.27 mm)
(-- cal_probe_center.ngc:27.)
o<measure_shoulder> sub
  #<ms_cutdia>  = #1 (=0 cutting diameter mm - 0 takes it from the tool table)
  #<ms_shdia>   = #2 (=0 shoulder diameter mm - required, from the ER size)
  #<ms_clear>   = #3 (=2.0 gap between the cutter and the puck edge mm)
  #<ms_puckdia> = #4 (=25.27 puck diameter mm - measured 2026-08-03)
  #<ms_start>   = #5 (=20.0 height above the puck to start the search mm)
  #<ms_max>     = #6 (=40.0 max search down mm)
  #<ms_ffast>   = #7 (=0 find feed - 0 takes the probe screen's own #3004)
  #<ms_fslow>   = #8 (=0 latch feed - 0 takes the probe screen's own #3005)
  #<ms_ftrav>   = #9 (=0 traverse feed - 0 takes the probe screen's own #3006)
  (2 mm, NOT 1. Operator 2026-08-13: "lets add an additional 1mm to the)
  (distance where it moves out. its too tight and we have some misalignment".)
  (The sideways step becomes 12.635 + 4.7625 + 2 = 19.3975 mm. It costs)
  (overlap: the nut still lands on the puck by shoulder radius minus cutting)
  (radius minus this gap = 14 - 4.7625 - 2 = 7.2375 mm, well clear of the)
  (0.5 mm refusal.)
  o1 if [#<ms_clear> LE 0]
    #<ms_clear> = 2.0
  o1 endif
  o2 if [#<ms_puckdia> LE 0]
    #<ms_puckdia> = 25.27
  o2 endif
  o3 if [#<ms_start> LE 0]
    #<ms_start> = 20.0
  o3 endif
  o4 if [#<ms_max> LE 0]
    #<ms_max> = 40.0
  o4 endif
  (FEEDS COME FROM THE PROBE SCREEN, NOT FROM THIS FILE. #3004/#3005/#3006)
  (are the same three cells tool_touch_off reads, so both probes move at one)
  (set of numbers and changing the screen changes both.)
  (2026-08-13: these used to default to invented constants 200/20/1000 while)
  (the screen said 600/50/8000 -- the find probe ran at 200 x 0.75 = 150 and)
  (the traverse at 1000, so the whole cycle crawled. Operator: "the fast probe)
  (is way too slow ... the shit was crawling".)
  o5 if [#<ms_ffast> LE 0]
    #<ms_ffast> = #3004
  o5 endif
  o6 if [#<ms_fslow> LE 0]
    #<ms_fslow> = #3005
  o6 endif
  o7 if [#<ms_ftrav> LE 0]
    #<ms_ftrav> = #3006
  o7 endif
  (75 PERCENT OF A ZERO-OFFSET PROBE, ALWAYS. Operator 2026-08-13: "make)
  (offset probes 75% of a zero offset probe". This probe is off-centre by)
  (construction -- the whole point is to miss the puck with the cutter -- and)
  (an off-axis touch bends the plate before it trips, so the reading walks)
  (with the approach speed. 75 PERCENT OF THE SCREEN VALUE, which is what a)
  (centred probe gets: 600 -> 450 find, 50 -> 37.5 latch. The traverse is not)
  (a probe and is not derated.)
  #<ms_ffast> = [#<ms_ffast> * 0.75]
  #<ms_fslow> = [#<ms_fslow> * 0.75]
  o10 if [#<_metric> EQ 0]
    (abort, MEASURE SHOULDER: switch to MM first)
  o10 endif
  o11 if [#5400 LE 0]
    (abort, MEASURE SHOULDER: no tool in the spindle - load the tool first, then press MEASURE TOOL OFFSET before this)
  o11 endif
  o12 if [#<ms_cutdia> LE 0]
    #<ms_cutdia> = #5410
  o12 endif
  o13 if [#<ms_cutdia> LE 0]
    (abort, MEASURE SHOULDER: T#5400 has no DIAMETER in the tool table. Fill in the DIAMETER MM cell on the TOOL tab and press this again.)
  o13 endif
  (EACH MISSING NUMBER GETS ITS OWN MESSAGE -- operator 2026-08-13: refuse)
  (loudly if anything is missing, naming the tool diameter or the collet)
  (diameter specifically. A single vague refusal makes the operator hunt.)
  o14 if [#<ms_shdia> LE 0]
    (abort, MEASURE SHOULDER: T#5400 has no SHOULDER DIA. Double-click that cell on the TOOL tab and pick the ER collet size - it is what decides how far the spindle steps off the puck.)
  o14 endif
  (CUTTER AT LEAST AS WIDE AS THE NUT: NOTHING TO PROBE, AND NOTHING MOVES.)
  (Operator 2026-08-13: "if cutter diameter is larger than the collet nut the)
  (probe is skipped and results are just entered so that shoulder = nose.)
  (effectively cutter is a solid shaft at cutting diameter all the way up.)
  (pressing probe will just enter that result into table instead of probing".)
  (The nut can never touch the work before the cutter body does, so the first)
  (thing that would collide is the cutter itself, all the way to the spindle)
  (nose. The shoulder therefore sits AT the nose: nose-to-nut is zero and the)
  (stickout is the whole tool length.)
  (-1, NOT 0, IS THE SENTINEL. #3100 reads 0 when nothing has ever been)
  (measured, so a plain zero could not be told apart from "never ran". -1)
  (says "measured, and the answer is that there is no nut in the way".)
  (THIS RUNS BEFORE THE PUCK DEPLOYS AND BEFORE ANY MOTION -- the button just)
  (writes the answer.)
  o19 if [#<ms_shdia> LE #<ms_cutdia>]
    #3100 = -1.0
    (PRINT, MEASURE SHOULDER: cutter #<ms_cutdia> is not narrower than the nut #<ms_shdia> - treating the tool as a solid shaft to the spindle nose. Nothing probed, nothing moved.)
    o<measure_shoulder> return
  o19 endif
  (NO #3047 GUARD. The puck record is no longer read by this routine at all -)
  (the toolsetter is located from G30 and the probe is relative - so gating on)
  (it would refuse for a reason that no longer exists. No contact is caught by)
  (the G38.2 guards below, which is the honest test.)
  o25 if [#3010 EQ 0]
    (abort, MEASURE SHOULDER: the spindle nose height #3010 is not set, so a shoulder height cannot be measured against it. Press FIND NOSE on the PROBING tab first.)
  o25 endif

  (SIDESTEP. Negative X so the move is away from the operator side, matching)
  (every other toolsetter approach on this machine.)
  #<ms_off> = [[#<ms_puckdia> / 2.0] + [#<ms_cutdia> / 2.0] + #<ms_clear>]
  #<ms_over> = [[#<ms_shdia> / 2.0] - [#<ms_cutdia> / 2.0] - #<ms_clear>]
  o16 if [#<ms_over> LE 0.5]
    (abort, MEASURE SHOULDER: the nut only overhangs the cutter by #<ms_over> mm - it lands on the puck edge or misses it. Fit a bigger ER nut or cut the clearance #<ms_clear> mm.)
  o16 endif
  (PRINT, MEASURE SHOULDER: cutter #<ms_cutdia> shoulder #<ms_shdia>, stepping #<ms_off> mm in -X, nut overlaps the puck top by #<ms_over> mm)

  G90
  M5
  M64 P3            (deploy the toolsetter puck)
  G4 P1.5
  (ALREADY AT G30? THEN DO NOT LIFT AND COME BACK -- same rule as)
  (tool_touch_off. Machine position is not a parameter, so it is rebuilt)
  (from the work position, the active G5x origin, G92 and the tool length.)
  (G5x origins sit one 20-slot block apart from 5221, and #5220 says which)
  (frame is live, so the base is 5201 + 20 times the frame number.)
  #<gbase> = [5201 + [#5220 * 20]]
  #<mach_x> = [#5420 + #[#<gbase>] + #5211]
  #<mach_y> = [#5421 + #[#<gbase> + 1] + #5212]
  #<mach_z> = [#5422 + #[#<gbase> + 2] + #5213 + #5403]
  #<at_g30> = 0
  o30 if [ABS[#<mach_x> - #5181] LT 0.1]
    o31 if [ABS[#<mach_y> - #5182] LT 0.1]
      o32 if [ABS[#<mach_z> - #5183] LT 0.1]
        #<at_g30> = 1
      o32 endif
    o31 endif
  o30 endif
  (THE TOOLSETTER IS AT G30, IN MACHINE COORDINATES -- #5181/#5182/#5183, the)
  (same three numbers tool_touch_off uses and the reason that routine works.)
  (2026-08-13: this used to reach for #3045/#3046 under G53. Those are)
  (WORK-frame numbers -- cal_probe_center.ngc:13 "Results, CURRENT WORK)
  (FRAME: #3045 = centre X" -- so G53 X[3668.5236 - 18.3975] asked for)
  (X3650.126 against an X MAX_LIMIT of 1.0. Both branches died on it with)
  ("Linear move would exceed X's positive limit", at G30 or not, which is why)
  (standing on G30 made no difference.)
  o33 if [#<at_g30> GT 0]
    (PRINT, MEASURE SHOULDER: already at G30 - stepping across and probing from here)
    G53 G1 F[#<ms_ftrav>] X[#5181 - #<ms_off>] Y#5182
  o33 else
    G53 G1 F[#<ms_ftrav>] Z0
    G53 G1 F[#<ms_ftrav>] X[#5181 - #<ms_off>] Y#5182
    G53 G1 F[#<ms_ftrav>] Z#5183
  o33 endif

  (PROBE THE WAY tool_touch_off DOES: RELATIVE, never an absolute Z target.)
  (#3007 is its max search, #3009 its retract - one probe screen, one set of)
  (numbers. An absolute target needs a frame; a G91 move needs none.)
  #<z_start> = #5422
  G91
  G38.2 Z-[#3007] F[#<ms_ffast>]
  G90
  o20 if [#5070 EQ 0]
    G53 G1 F[#<ms_ftrav>] Z0
    M65 P3
    (abort, MEASURE SHOULDER: no contact - the shoulder never reached the puck. Check the ER size and that the tool is seated)
  o20 endif
  G91
  G1 Z[#3009] F[#<ms_ftrav>]
  G38.2 Z-[#3009 * 2.0] F[#<ms_fslow>]
  G90
  o21 if [#5070 EQ 0]
    G53 G1 F[#<ms_ftrav>] Z0
    M65 P3
    (abort, MEASURE SHOULDER: slow latch found nothing - the first touch was not repeatable)
  o21 endif

  (RESULT PARAMETER: #3100, AND ONLY THE LENGTH.)
  (WHY NOT #3011 and #3012: tool_touch_off.ngc:17-18 owns those as)
  (tool_diameter_probe_mode and tool_diameter_offset_mode.)
  (WHY NOT THE 303x PAIR EITHER -- my own mistake, 2026-08-13. I picked them)
  (by grepping the ned g-code tree, where they looked free. They are not:)
  (Probe Basic binds them to probe-screen WIDGETS -- probe_basic_ui.py:8048)
  ("breakage_tolerance_3037" and :8128 "user_setter_1_3038". They held 2.0 and)
  (0.001, so the GUI harvested a shoulder of tool length minus 2.0 and a)
  (diameter of 0.001, which printed as "0.0" over the operator's ER16-28.)
  (#3100 sits outside Probe Basic's whole 3000-3098 widget block and is)
  (referenced nowhere else in this tree. It carries ONE number.)
  (THE DIAMETER IS NOT WRITTEN BY ANYTHING HERE. Operator 2026-08-13: "no one)
  (but user should touch shoulder diameter" ... "code can only touch shoulder)
  (length offset. nothing else". It arrives as an INPUT from the ER cell and)
  (goes back nowhere.)
  #3100 = [ABS[#3010 + #5063 - #<z_start>]]
  (PRINT, MEASURE SHOULDER: nut face sits #3100 mm below the spindle nose - written to #3100. The diameter is yours and is not touched.)

  (BACK TO G30, DIRECT -- same rule as tool_touch_off. Operator 2026-08-13:)
  ("if we are starting from after tool probe, a linear move to the m30)
  (location is fine". G30 Z is -344.572, BELOW the puck top -272.733, so the)
  (old lift to machine Z0 climbed 344 mm only to come back down past where it)
  (started. The puck drops first and gets the deploy's own 1.5 s, because it)
  (is the only thing between the tool and G30 Z.)
  M65 P3            (retract the puck)
  G4 P1.5
  G53 G1 F[#<ms_ftrav>] X#5181 Y#5182 Z#5183
o<measure_shoulder> endsub
M2 (end program)
